Since the subsequence has only 5 elements, I can enumerate the multiset of the other four values by their counts. Concretely, for each middle index p (as a position in the original array), let L = positions before p, R = positions after p, and for each value x count cL[x] in L and cR[x] in R. A chosen set has a = number of v's among the 4 non-middle elements, and the rest are other values. Valid configurations: the count of v in the whole subsequence (1 + a) must be strictly greater than the count of any other value. With only 4 other slots, I'll enumerate by pair choices for the left (2 picks) and right (2 picks) grouped by value, counting with combinations over value-occurrence counts. This is O(n²) per middle with small constant work, which is fine for n=1000.

