class Solution:
    def minimumCost(self, nums: List[int], cost: List[int], k: int) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
This is a DP over partitions with a position-dependent subarray index i, so I'll set it up as a DP with the segment count dimension. Let P[r] = prefix sum of nums through r, C[r] = prefix sum of cost. A subarray [l..r] as the i-th segment costs (P[r] + k·i) · (C[r] − C[l−1]).

Define dp[i][r] = min cost to partition nums[0..r] into i segments. Then dp[i][r] = min over l of dp[i−1][l−1] + (P[r] + k·i)·(C[r] − C[l−1]). Expanding gives a linear function in C[l−1] for fixed i, so this is a convex-hull trick with O(n²·n) naive cost. With n ≤ 1000, the naive O(n² · n) is 10⁹, too slow in Python, so I'll use the usual observation: the number of segments is at most n, and per i the transition is O(n²), totaling O(n³) — still too much. Instead, since the segment index multiplies only the (P[r] + k·i) term, I'll use the standard 1D DP: let dp[r] be over all segment counts, with the segment index folded in by noting the cost term k·i·(C[r]−C[l−1]) makes i depend on the count, which requires the count dimension.

Given the time, I'll implement the O(n²·segments-bounded) DP directly with the count dimension and rely on the input size being moderate, then reason through example 1 to check it.
