class Solution:
    def beautifulNumbers(self, l: int, r: int) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
Plan, following the process I just committed to:

Digit DP over numbers up to 10^9. The state needs the digit sum (up to 81) and the product of digits. The product is at most 9^9, which is too many values to track directly, so I'll use the standard reduction: the product of digits is only relevant modulo the digit sum. I'll track (position, tight, sum so far, product mod S) for each target digit sum S from 1 to 81, and at the end check whether product mod S == 0 with sum == S. The product of digits is 0 if any digit is 0; I'll track a "has zero" flag, since then product = 0 which is divisible by any sum.

Hand-check against examples: 10 → product 0, sum 1, 0 divisible by 1 ✓. 20 → product 0 ✓. 12 → product 2, sum 3, not divisible ✗. Range 1..15 gives 1..9 (product = digit, sum = digit ✓) and 10 (product 0 ✓), and 11 (product 1, sum 2 ✗), 12–15 ✗. Total 10 ✓.

Count f(x) = beautiful numbers in [0, x], answer = f(r) − f(l−1). Zero has sum 0, so I'll exclude it by only counting numbers with sum ≥ 1 (it has sum 0, which I skip when checking divisibility).
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "T-6153", "code": "from functools import lru_cache\n\nclass Solution:\n    def beautifulNumbers(self, l: int, r: int) -> int:\n        def count_upto(x: int) -> int:\n            # counts beautiful numbers in [1, x]\n            if x <= 0:\n                return 0\n            digits = list(map(int, str(x)))\n            n = len(digits)\n            total = 0\n            # For each target digit sum S in 1..81, count numbers <= x with digit sum S\n            # and product divisible by S, using DP over (pos, tight, sum, prod mod S, has_zero).\n            for S in range(1, 9 * n + 1):\n                @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n                def dp(pos, tight, s, pm, has_zero, started):\n                    if s > S:\n                        return 0\n                    if pos == n:\n                        if not started or s != S:\n                            return 0\n                        if has_zero:\n                            return 1  # product 0 divisible by S\n                        return 1 if pm == 0 else 0\n                    lim = digits[pos] if tight else 9\n                    res = 0\n                    for d in range(lim + 1):\n                        nt = tight and d == lim\n                        if not started and d == 0:\n                            res += dp(pos + 1, nt, 0, 1 % S, False, False)\n                        else:\n                            nz = has_zero or d == 0\n                            npm = (pm * d) % S\n                            res += dp(pos + 1, nt, s + d, npm, nz, True)\n                    return res\n                total += dp(0, True, 0, 1 % S, False, False)\n                dp.cache_clear()\n            return total\n\n        return count_upto(r) - count_upto(l - 1)\n"}

