2 <= n == fruits.length == fruits[i].length <= 1000
0 <= fruits[i][j] <= 1000

Starter code:

class Solution:
    def maxCollectedFruits(self, fruits: List[List[int]]) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
Plan, following my process:

This is the three-children variant of the classic problem. Two of the children's paths are each constrained so they can meet, which is what makes it tractable.

Key structure: child A from (0,0) moves diagonally-forward (row+1 and column changes of +1, 0, or +1 in its own frame). Child B from (0,n−1) and child C from (n−1,0) have symmetric structure. Standard solution:
- Child A's path is independent: it just collects the diagonal region above the main diagonal's cells it passes through. Its total is the sum of fruits along its path, and the cells in the strictly upper triangle (j > i) are reachable only for B's region, and cells with i > j only for C's. The main diagonal cells are shared by A.
- Simplest correct method: take child A's best path via DP in the grid, then for B and C use DP restricted to the lower/upper triangles, with the diagonal cell (n−1,n−1) counted once.

Hand check on example 2 with n = 2: all four cells are used, total 4 ✓ (cells (0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (1,1) all collected once each).

I'll implement: total = sum of diagonal cells (i,i) for all i (taken by A along the main diagonal), plus the best path DP for B in the region j > i, plus the best path DP for C in the region i > j, with the (n−1,n−1) cell counted once, and the two DPs restricted so paths don't reuse cells.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "T-8444", "code": "from typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maxCollectedFruits(self, fruits: List[List[int]]) -> int:\n        n = len(fruits)\n        NEG = float('-inf')\n\n        # Child 1 from (0,0): always walks the main diagonal region; collects diagonal cells.\n        total = sum(fruits[i][i] for i in range(n))\n\n        # Child 2 from (0, n-1): restricted to the upper triangle (j > i), moves down.\n        # Child 3 from (n-1, 0): restricted to the lower triangle (i > j), moves right.\n        # Each collects cells strictly in their triangle, plus the shared corner (n-1,n-1)\n        # is handled once at the end.\n\n        def upper_best():\n            # Child from (0, n-1) moves (i+1, j-1), (i+1, j), (i+1, j+1); stays in j > i.\n            # DP over rows, column j in (i, n-1].\n            dp = [NEG] * n\n            dp[n - 1] = fruits[0][n - 1]\n            for i in range(1, n):\n                nd = [NEG] * n\n                for j in range(n):\n                    if j <= i:\n                        continue\n                    best = NEG\n                    for pj in (j - 1, j, j + 1):\n                        if 0 <= pj < n and dp[pj] > best:\n                            best = dp[pj]\n                    if best > NEG:\n                        nd[j] = best + fruits[i][j]\n                dp = nd\n            return dp[n - 1]\n\n        def lower_best():\n            # Child from (n-1, 0) moves (i-1, j+1), (i, j+1), (i+1, j+1); stays in i > j.\n            # Transpose the problem: swap roles.\n            transposed = [[fruits[j][i] for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]\n            dp = [NEG] * n\n            dp[n - 1] = transposed[0][n - 1]\n            for i in range(1, n):\n                nd = [NEG] * n\n                for j in range(n):\n                    if j <= i:\n                        continue\n                    best = NEG\n                    for pj in (j - 1, j, j + 1):\n                        if 0 <= pj < n and dp[pj] > best:\n                            best = dp[pj]\n                    if best > NEG:\n                        nd[j] = best + transposed[i][j]\n                dp = nd\n            return dp[n - 1]\n\n        # The corner (n-1, n-1) is on the diagonal and already counted once in `total`.\n        # Upper/lower DPs include only strictly-triangular cells plus their endpoint at corner\n        # (which we exclude to avoid double counting).\n        up = upper_best()\n        low = lower_best()\n        total += up - fruits[n - 1][n - 1] + low - fruits[n - 1][n - 1]\n        return total\n"}

