class Solution:
    def maxCollectedFruits(self, fruits: List[List[int]]) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
This is the classic "three children collecting fruits" problem. The standard solution:

1. The child from (0,0) moves diagonally-ish toward (n-1,n-1); its path is independent of the others' interference except at shared cells. Sum its diagonal cells: for each step t, it's at (t,t) by taking the main diagonal, which is the optimal sum for this child — collect `fruits[i][i]` for all i.
2. The child from (0, n-1) moves down-left-to-diagonal and the child from (n-1, 0) moves right-up-to-diagonal. Both are handled with DP restricted to the region strictly above/below the main diagonal, respectively, to avoid double counting cells on the diagonal (already counted by child 1).
3. For the upper-right triangle (j > i), run DP for child 2 from (0, n-1) with moves (i+1, j-1), (i+1, j), (i+1, j+1), restricted to cells with j > i, and similarly for child 3 from (n-1, 0) in the lower-left triangle (i > j) with moves (i-1, j+1), (i, j+1), (i+1, j+1) transposed.
4. Add the three totals.

