class Solution:
    def countSubstrings(self, s: str) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
A substring ending at index `j` with last digit `d = s[j]` (non-zero) is divisible by `d` exactly when its numeric value mod `d` is 0. Since `d` is between 1 and 9, I can handle each last digit separately.

The approach: for each non-zero last digit `d`, I need to count pairs `(i, j)` with `i <= j`, `s[j] = d`, and `value(s[i..j]) ≡ 0 (mod d)`. Using prefix values `P[k] = value(s[0..k-1]) mod d` gives `value(s[i..j]) = P[j+1] - P[i] * 10^(j-i+1)`, which doesn't factor neatly, so instead I'll use the standard technique: for each `d`, maintain a running count over positions, updating the remainder of the substring ending at each position incrementally with a Horner-style scan from each start.

Given the length bound, I'll do the direct computation per end index: for each `j` with non-zero digit `d`, scan start indices leftward while accumulating the value mod `d`, counting zeros of the remainder. Each substring's value mod `d` can be built right-to-left using powers of 10 mod `d`.

