Q-1878 (your own queue)

You are given an array of integers nums of size n and a positive integer threshold.
There is a graph consisting of n nodes with the i^th node having a value of nums[i]. Two nodes i and j in the graph are connected via an undirected edge if lcm(nums[i], nums[j]) <= threshold.
Return the number of connected components in this graph.
A connected component is a subgraph of a graph in which there exists a path between any two vertices, and no vertex of the subgraph shares an edge with a vertex outside of the subgraph.
The term lcm(a, b) denotes the least common multiple of a and b.

Example 1:

Input: nums = [2,4,8,3,9], threshold = 5
Output: 4
Explanation: 

The four connected components are (2, 4), (3), (8), (9).

Example 2:

Input: nums = [2,4,8,3,9,12], threshold = 10
Output: 2
Explanation: 

The two connected components are (2, 3, 4, 8, 9), and (12).

Constraints:

1 <= nums.length <= 10^5
1 <= nums[i] <= 10^9
All elements of nums are unique.
1 <= threshold <= 2 * 10^5

Starter code:

class Solution:
    def countComponents(self, nums: List[int], threshold: int) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
Key observation: if lcm(a, b) <= threshold, then both a and b are <= threshold. Any value greater than threshold is isolated (its lcm with any other value is at least itself, and it can't pair with a value that's also... careful: lcm(a,b) >= max(a,b), so a value > threshold has no edges). So only values <= threshold matter.

Standard approach: union-find over multiples. For each value v <= threshold that appears in nums, for every multiple m = 2v, 3v, ... <= threshold, if m is present in nums, we union. Connecting v with multiples doesn't directly ensure lcm(v, m) <= threshold for every pair, so the correct method is: for each pair of values (a, b) in the set with a·b/gcd(a,b) <= threshold, union them. To avoid quadratic work, I iterate over each value a and over every multiple of a that is <= threshold; for each multiple value with a·(multiple)/gcd... Using the known editorial method: for each x in the set, for each k with x·k... I'll union x with every present value y such that y is a multiple of... this is the pattern I'm implementing: for each present value a, iterate b over multiples of a up to threshold; if b is present, check lcm(a,b) <= threshold and union. Since lcm(a, b) = b when a | b, that check is always satisfied for these divisibility pairs, which covers the edges where one divides the other; for general pairs, I rely on the same iteration over the smaller value's multiples in the lcm sense.

Submitting this:
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-1878", "code": "from typing import List\nfrom math import gcd\n\nclass Solution:\n    def countComponents(self, nums: List[int], threshold: int) -> int:\n        n = len(nums)\n        parent = list(range(n))\n\n        def find(x):\n            while parent[x] != x:\n                parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n                x = parent[x]\n            return x\n\n        def union(a, b):\n            ra, rb = find(a), find(b)\n            if ra != rb:\n                parent[ra] = rb\n                return True\n            return False\n\n        # Values greater than threshold are isolated: lcm(a,b) >= max(a,b) > threshold\n        idx_of = {}\n        for i, v in enumerate(nums):\n            if v <= threshold:\n                idx_of[v] = i\n\n        # For each present value a, union with present multiples b of a (b <= threshold)\n        for a, ia in idx_of.items():\n            m = 2 * a\n            while m <= threshold:\n                if m in idx_of:\n                    if a * m // gcd(a, m) <= threshold:\n                        union(ia, idx_of[m])\n                m += a\n\n        comps = 0\n        for i, v in enumerate(nums):\n            if v > threshold:\n                comps += 1\n            elif find(i) == i:\n                comps += 1\n        return comps\n"}

