class Solution:
    def maxSubarraySum(self, nums: List[int], k: int) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
Approach: with prefix sums P, a subarray (i, j] has length j−i divisible by k exactly when i ≡ j (mod k). So for each residue class, track the minimum prefix sum seen so far at indices with that residue, and for each j, candidate = P[j] − min over earlier i with i ≡ j mod k.

Check example 3: nums=[-5,1,2,-3,4], k=2, P=[0,-5,-4,-2,-5,-1]. For j=4 (P=-5), residue 0; earlier residue-0 indices: 0 (P=0), 2 (P=-4). min=-4, candidate = -5-(-4) = -1. For j=5 (P=-1), residue 1; earlier residue-1: 1 (P=-5), 3 (P=-2); min=-5, candidate=4. ✓ Max is 4.

Example 2: k=4, nums all negative; subarray of length 4 from start gives -10. The method covers this since it enumerates all valid lengths.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-3727", "code": "from typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maxSubarraySum(self, nums: List[int], k: int) -> int:\n        n = len(nums)\n        INF = float('inf')\n        min_pref = [INF] * k\n        # prefix index 0\n        min_pref[0] = 0\n        P = 0\n        best = -INF\n        for j in range(1, n + 1):\n            P += nums[j - 1]\n            r = j % k\n            if min_pref[r] != INF:\n                cand = P - min_pref[r]\n                if cand > best:\n                    best = cand\n            if P < min_pref[r]:\n                min_pref[r] = P\n        return best\n"}

