=== MODEL ===
Plan: fix the middle pair (q, r) with r − q ≥ 2. Then p ranges over indices ≤ q−2 and s over indices ≥ r+2. The condition nums[p]·nums[r] == nums[q]·nums[s] becomes nums[p]/nums[q] == nums[s]/nums[r], so I count fractions in reduced form. For each q, I keep a counter of reduced ratios nums[p]/nums[q] for all valid p, growing as q advances. For each r, I need the counts of nums[s]/nums[r] for s ≥ r+2, which I'll precompute as a suffix counter and remove from as r moves.

Tracing example 1 ([1,2,3,4,3,6,1]): the only matching quadruple is (0,2,4,6), which the scheme counts once, and the others don't match, giving 1 ✓.

I'll implement it with a suffix counter over s and a prefix counter over p, using gcd-reduced fractions as keys.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-8357", "code": "from typing import List\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nfrom math import gcd\n\nclass Solution:\n    def numberOfSubsequences(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:\n        n = len(nums)\n\n        def frac(a, b):\n            g = gcd(a, b)\n            return (a // g, b // g)\n\n        # suffix counter of ratios nums[s]/nums[r] for s >= start\n        # We iterate r; the valid s are s >= r + 2.\n        suffix = defaultdict(int)\n        # Initially include s >= 2 (for r = 0 we need s >= 2), we'll add as r changes.\n        # Start with r = 2 region: suffix holds s in [r+2, n-1].\n        # Build for r = 2 initially: s from 4 to n-1.\n        for s in range(4, n):\n            suffix[nums[s]] += 0  # placeholder to keep structure simple\n        # Simpler: recompute counts per r via a counter of values in suffix range.\n        # Use value counts; ratio key depends on nums[r], so we use value counts directly.\n        valcount = defaultdict(int)\n        for s in range(4, n):\n            valcount[nums[s]] += 1\n\n        total = 0\n        # prefix counter over p: counts of values nums[p] for p <= q - 2\n        prefix_val = defaultdict(int)\n\n        for q in range(1, n - 2):\n            # add p = q - 2 into prefix if valid\n            if q - 2 >= 0:\n                prefix_val[nums[q - 2]] += 1\n            for r in range(q + 2, n - 1):\n                # ensure valcount holds s in [r+2, n-1]\n                # remove s = r+1 ... handled by maintaining as r increases below\n                pass\n            # Recompute properly: iterate r with s-range maintained.\n            pass\n\n        # Clean, correct implementation below (overwrites the above).\n        total = 0\n        for q in range(1, n - 2):\n            # prefix values for p in [0, q-2]\n            pref = defaultdict(int)\n            for p in range(0, q - 1):\n                pref[nums[p]] += 1\n            # for each r in [q+2, n-3]; s in [r+2, n-1]\n            for r in range(q + 2, n - 2):\n                # counts of s in [r+2, n-1] by value\n                suf = defaultdict(int)\n                for s in range(r + 2, n):\n                    suf[nums[s]] += 1\n                # need nums[p]*nums[r] == nums[q]*nums[s]\n                # for each p, count s with nums[s] == nums[p]*nums[r]/nums[q]\n                for pv, pc in pref.items():\n                    num = pv * nums[r]\n                    den = nums[q]\n                    if num % den == 0:\n                        sv = num // den\n                        if sv in suf:\n                            total += pc * suf[sv]\n        return total\n"}

