Let me redo it properly. The key idea stays the same: for each middle pair (q, r) with r ≥ q+2, count pairs (p, s) with p ≤ q−2, s ≥ r+2, and nums[p]·nums[r] == nums[q]·nums[s]. To make it fast, I'll keep the prefix counts over p incrementally as q grows, and keep a suffix count over s that shrinks as r grows, with a fixed q. For each (q, r), I'll iterate over distinct p-values, which is at most 1000 distinct values, so the work is about n² × 1000 in the worst case. That's too slow in Python for the full bound, so I'll use the reduced-fraction form instead: key on nums[p]/nums[q] for the prefix side and nums[s]/nums[r] for the suffix side, which means each (q, r) needs only a dictionary lookup.

