Approach: sort indices by nums1. Process groups of equal nums1 values in increasing order. For each group, the answer is the sum of the k largest nums2 values among all strictly smaller nums1 entries. Keep a min-heap of the top k values seen so far plus a running sum; when a new value arrives, push it and pop if the heap exceeds k. Before adding a group's elements, record the current sum for each index in that group.

Tracing example 1: nums1=[4,2,1,5,3], nums2=[10,20,30,40,50], k=2. Index 2 (nums1=1) has nothing smaller, so 0 ✓. Index 1 (nums1=2): smaller set {index 2 value 30}, sum 30 ✓. Index 0 (nums1=4): smaller set {30,20,50} → top 2 = 80 ✓.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-8777", "code": "from typing import List\nimport heapq\n\nclass Solution:\n    def findMaxSum(self, nums1: List[int], nums2: List[int], k: int) -> List[int]:\n        n = len(nums1)\n        order = sorted(range(n), key=lambda i: nums1[i])\n        ans = [0] * n\n        heap = []  # min-heap of the top-k nums2 values among processed smaller elements\n        heap_sum = 0\n        i = 0\n        while i < n:\n            j = i\n            # group of equal nums1 values\n            while j < n and nums1[order[j]] == nums1[order[i]]:\n                j += 1\n            # record answers for this group using only strictly smaller elements\n            for t in range(i, j):\n                ans[order[t]] = heap_sum\n            # now add this group's nums2 values into the heap\n            for t in range(i, j):\n                v = nums2[order[t]]\n                if len(heap) < k:\n                    heapq.heappush(heap, v)\n                    heap_sum += v\n                elif v > heap[0]:\n                    heap_sum += v - heapq.heapreplace(heap, v)\n            i = j\n        return ans\n"}

