Correct model: for each index i, we need nums[i] distinct queries covering i, each used at most once per index, and each query can decrement any subset of its range. The right formulation is a sweep with a max-heap, but the assignment isn't a per-position greedy with returns. The standard solution: sweep positions left to right; at position i, add queries starting at i into a max-heap keyed by right endpoint. Discard heap entries whose r < i. Then pop the top nums[i] queries (largest r, which reach furthest) and mark each as used for one decrement at i. Queries are consumed when used, and each query is used at most once per index, so it's a one-shot use per position, not a pool that returns. Remaining queries stay available for later positions, but popped ones are consumed for this position only, so they must be pushed back only if their r ≥ i+1 (they can cover later indices, and each later index needs a fresh use). A query used at i is still usable at i+1, so pushing back is correct for the "each query once per index" constraint.

Checking example 2: nums=[4,3,2,1], queries [1,3],[0,2]. At i=0, active is only [0,2] (r=2), heap size 1 < 4 → false ✓.

The previous attempt used the same max-heap idea but popped the smallest-r queries (heapq is a min-heap on negated r, so popping gives largest r, which is what I want). The real bug was in how I counted queries used per index: I used `len(heap) < need` but the heap also held queries with r < i that had not yet been removed in some cases, and I removed them only from the top. I'll rebuild the heap cleanly with explicit filtering each step.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-5886", "code": "from typing import List\nimport heapq\n\nclass Solution:\n    def isZeroArray(self, nums: List[int], queries: List[List[int]]) -> bool:\n        n = len(nums)\n        starts = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n        for l, r in queries:\n            starts[l].append(r)\n        heap = []  # min-heap of negated right endpoints (max-heap by r)\n        for i in range(n):\n            for r in starts[i]:\n                heapq.heappush(heap, -r)\n            # discard queries that end before i\n            while heap and -heap[0] < i:\n                heapq.heappop(heap)\n            need = nums[i]\n            if need == 0:\n                continue\n            if len(heap) < need:\n                return False\n            # use the `need` queries that reach furthest right\n            used = [heapq.heappop(heap) for _ in range(need)]\n            # each used query can serve later positions too (once per index)\n            for x in used:\n                if -x > i:\n                    heapq.heappush(heap, x)\n        return True\n"}

