Starter code:

class Solution:
    def maxFrequency(self, nums: List[int], k: int, numOperations: int) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
Plan for this one: we pick a target value t, and up to numOperations elements can be shifted into [t−k, t+k]. Elements already equal to t need no operation. The frequency of t is (count of nums equal to t) + min(numOperations, number of other elements within distance k of t). Maximize over candidate targets.

Candidate targets are the values nums[i]±k and nums[i] themselves, which covers the optimal t. I'll use a sorted array and binary search to count elements in [t−k, t+k], and a Counter for the exact matches.

Checking example 1: nums=[1,4,5], k=1, numOps=2. Target t=4: exact count 1, elements in [3,5] excluding exact: {5} → 1. Frequency = 1 + min(2,1) = 2 ✓. Example 2: nums=[5,11,20,20], k=5, numOps=1. Target 20: exact 2, others in [15,25] none → 2 ✓.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-1885", "code": "from typing import List\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom bisect import bisect_left, bisect_right\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maxFrequency(self, nums: List[int], k: int, numOperations: int) -> int:\n        nums_sorted = sorted(nums)\n        cnt = Counter(nums_sorted)\n        candidates = set()\n        for x in nums_sorted:\n            candidates.add(x)\n            candidates.add(x - k)\n            candidates.add(x + k)\n        best = 0\n        for t in candidates:\n            exact = cnt.get(t, 0)\n            lo = bisect_left(nums_sorted, t - k)\n            hi = bisect_right(nums_sorted, t + k)\n            in_range = hi - lo\n            others = in_range - exact\n            freq = exact + min(numOperations, others)\n            if freq > best:\n                best = freq\n        return best\n"}

